  <main id="main" class="container">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Executive summary · submission workflow</p>
      <h1>How to create, validate, and submit a GEMS GeoTIFF.</h1>
      <p class="lede">The process below is the exact safe path from authorized competition data to an upload. The first step today is to stop: this checkout has no validated file.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="status-card" style="margin-bottom:32px" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="status-top"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span data-submission-status>NOT READY TO SUBMIT</span></div>
      <p data-submission-reason>No validated GeoTIFF is available in GEMSDOE87. Do not download or upload an unvalidated placeholder.</p>
      <span class="no-download">Download disabled until all gates pass</span>
      <a class="button hidden" data-submission-download href="#" download>Download validated submission</a>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
      <div class="alert"><strong>Current disposition: blocked.</strong> The repository does not contain the competition feature/label rasters, official sample submission, cached feature stack, shared holdout evaluator/writer, registry rasters, or a generated candidate. No score, correlation, overlap, or TIFF-validation result is claimed.</div>

      <h2>Official submission format</h2>
      <p>Submit one GeoTIFF with one raster band, 32-bit floating point predictions, and the exact CRS, shape, and affine transform of the competition's sample submission. Values must be probabilities or confidence scores in the closed interval [0, 1]; pixels outside the study footprint must be null/NaN. The official sample file is the authoritative grid template.</p>

      <h2>Submission steps once the gates pass</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Get the authorized competition files.</strong> Sign in to the official <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DrivenData data tab</a> and download the supplied training features, labels, sample submission, and any linked DEM index required by the chosen method. Keep credentials out of Git and do not upload private data to this site. The unauthenticated session here redirected to sign-in; it did not retrieve the protected files.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the shared template, not a private reimplementation.</strong> Restore the cached feature stack and shared <code>evaluate_holdout.py</code> / <code>submission_writer.py</code>. The evaluator must hide whole fault segments with a buffer, compute catalogue features from visible faults only, mask visible labels pixel-exactly, and pool the distance-weighted Tversky index. This checkout currently does not contain those shared files.</li>
        <li><strong>Prove the candidate on blocked holdout first.</strong> Run each feature alone as a leakage canary; investigate any AUC above 0.90. Report HOLDOUT-DTI with evaluator version, withheld-positive count, and 95% CI. Require the paired improvement over the current holdout best to be positive with an interval supporting improvement. Do not select a weekly submission slot based on a projection or an unverified sibling-site score.</li>
        <li><strong>Check uniqueness before and after placement.</strong> Compare the candidate surface rank-correlation and final dot overlap to every available registry raster. Stop and log a duplicate if rank-correlation exceeds 0.90 or if more than 70% of the emitted dots lie within 3 pixels of a registry-dot raster. This check is not possible until the registry is restored.</li>
        <li><strong>Write one GeoTIFF from the official sample grid.</strong> Use the shared writer and the exact sample CRS, shape, and affine transform. The submission format requires one band, float32 values in <code>[0, 1]</code>, and null/NaN outside the footprint. Never copy a previous submission. Use a unique name and a short, reproducible note of at most 140 characters.</li>
        <li><strong>Run an independent byte-level preflight.</strong> Reopen the written TIFF from disk with <code>scripts/validate_submission.py</code>, compare against the official sample grid, and save its JSON receipt. The validator checks band count, float32, CRS, dimensions, transform/bounds, no non-finite values inside the footprint, finite values in range (including nodata sentinels), and null/nodata outside the footprint. Example after the files are present:<br><code>python scripts/validate_submission.py --prediction path/to/candidate.tif --reference data/sample_submission.tif --report evidence/validation.json</code></li>
        <li><strong>Only then present a download.</strong> The landing page reads <code>data/submission-status.json</code> and only enables a TIFF download when the run card, all required gates, a unique name/note, and a 64-character SHA-256 receipt are present. The current status file intentionally has no artifact or download URL.</li>
        <li><strong>Upload through the competition portal.</strong> In DrivenData, choose the single-band <code>.tif</code> file, enter the recorded unique submission name and note, submit, and save the organizer result/receipt. The prize rules allow multiple feedback submissions subject to the live weekly limit, but require choosing one final entry for evaluation across both prize phases. A participant account and portal upload remain necessary; this project does not collect account credentials.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Why the previous “Predicted values must be in range [0, 1]” error can happen</h2>
      <p>The competition page requires predictions in the unit interval. The error means at least one value read by the portal was outside that range; plausible causes include a finite nodata sentinel such as a large negative float, unnormalized feature values written through as predictions, or an invalid cast. NaN handling and nodata tags must also match the template. The exact rejected file is unavailable here, so no cause is claimed as proven. The validator intentionally checks every finite cell, including finite nodata sentinels, and fails closed.</p>

      <h2>What to put in the portal's Note field</h2>
      <p>Use one concise, reproducible statement (maximum 140 characters), for example: <code>CMGEP holdout-v1; scale-persistent magnetic/gravity edges; paired spatial holdout; see run card</code>. This is an example note only—not a result and not associated with a downloadable file.</p>
      <p><strong>AI disclosure:</strong> the September 2026 official rules allow generative AI use but require disclosure in the submission narrative and keep the entrant responsible for accuracy and authorship. Document AI assistance truthfully if this work is used in an entry.</p>

      <h2>Official references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/967/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Problem description, metric, and GeoTIFF format</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Competition data tab</a> (sign-in may be required)</li>
        <li><a href="https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/96647.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE/NLR official rules PDF</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/drivendataorg/gems-prize-reference-solution" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official reference solution</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small">The rules, weekly cap, and deadline can change. Re-check official materials at the time of submission. The current status and machine-readable run card are linked from the <a href="index.html">project overview</a>.</p>
    </section>
